## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical, scalable cache invalidation across services, covering Redis pub/sub, versioned keys, and cross-tenant isolation.

## Key Concepts

- Cache-aside vs write-through
- Versioned keys: item:{id}:v and item:{id}
- Redis pub/sub for coordinated invalidation
- Stampede prevention: refresh locks and TTL tuning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Write path (pseudo)
function updateItem(id, value){
  const vKey = `item:${id}:v`;
  const valueKey = `item:${id}`;
  const newV = Date.now();
  redis.incr(vKey);
  redis.set(valueKey, value, 'EX', 60);
  redis.publish('invalidation', JSON.stringify({id, v:newV}));
}
```

```javascript
// Read path (pseudo)
async function readItem(id){
  const vKey = `item:${id}:v`;
  const valueKey = `item:${id}`;
  const currentV = await redis.get(vKey) || 0;
  let value = await cache.get(valueKey);
  if(!value){
     value = db.query(id);
     cache.set(valueKey, value, 60);
  }
  const cachedV = parseInt(await redis.get(vKey) || '0', 10);
  if(currentV > cachedV){
     value = db.query(id);
     cache.set(valueKey, value, 60);
     redis.set(vKey, currentV);
  }
  return value;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you tune TTLs for latency vs staleness across tenants?
- How would you rotate keys and migrate tenants without downtime?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design distributed workflow using sagas, CQRS, and event-sourcing in real-world microservices; probes handling of failure modes, idempotency, compensations, and consistency guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration vs choreography
- Event store and projections for CQRS
- Compensating actions and timeouts
- Idempotent handlers and outbox pattern

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: saga orchestrator pattern
class CheckoutSaga {
  async start(order) {
    await dispatch('ReserveInventory', order);
  }
  async onEvent(event) {
    if (event.type === 'InventoryReserved') await dispatch('CapturePayment', event.order);
    else if (event.type === 'PaymentCaptured') await dispatch('ScheduleShipment', event.order);
    else if (event.type === 'ShipmentScheduled') complete(event.order);
    else if (event.type.endsWith('Failed')) compensate(event.order);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What are the trade-offs between orchestration and choreography in this scenario?
- How would you test the saga under partial failures and timeouts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests versioned cache invalidation across tenants, balancing consistency and hot-path performance.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant, per-product cache keys with a shared version
- Invalidation via version bump and pub/sub
- Stampede protection with distributed locking
- TTL-based fallback for eventual consistency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Python-like pseudocode in JS terms; Redis calls shown for clarity
async function updateProduct(pId, price, stock){
  const ver = await redis.incr(`product:${pId}:version`);
  await redis.publish(`prod_updated:${pId}`, String(ver));
}
async function getProductForTenant(pId, tenantId){
  const ver = await redis.get(`product:${pId}:version`);
  const cacheKey = `cache:prod:${pId}:tenant:${tenantId}:v${ver}`;
  let data = await redis.get(cacheKey);
  if(data) return JSON.parse(data);
  const lock = `lock:cache:${pId}:${tenantId}`;
  const ok = await redis.set(lock, '1', 'NX', 'EX', 5);
  if(ok){
    data = await loadFromDB(pId); // resp: price, stock, etc.
    await redis.set(cacheKey, JSON.stringify(data), 'EX', 60);
    await redis.del(lock);
  } else {
    await sleep(50);
    return getProductForTenant(pId, tenantId);
  }
  return data;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this approach across regions with cross-region cache coherence?
- How would you monitor invalidation latency and TTL tuning in production?

